<http://data.silknow.org/event/db0f12a7-7651-3227-8210-9eb176feb9eb>
        a                              ecrm:E8_Acquisition ;
        rdfs:comment                   "This carpet was initially on long term loan to the South Kensington Museum (today called the V&A), from 1883, and it would be over twenty years before the museum officially acquired it, in 1910. At that time, the owner was the art-collector George Salting, who loaned so much (particularly Chinese porcelain) to the museum, that a dedicated gallery was organised just to display his collection. After Salting’s death in 1909, the collection was bequeathed to the V&A on a permanent basis. The carpet was discussed as “the Salting Carpet” in later scholarship (see below), but it was not the only carpet in the Salting Bequest."@en ;
        ecrm:P14_carried_out_by        <http://data.silknow.org/actor/b5c41633-51a5-3e73-a33b-b68a11bcd8ec> ;
        ecrm:P22_transferred_title_to  <http://data.silknow.org/actor/f0577f91-f887-3019-bf88-f9e5ba019390> ;
        ecrm:P23_transferred_title_from
                "Bequeathed by George Salting" ;
        ecrm:P24_transferred_title_of  <http://data.silknow.org/object/a2687f43-081f-3be6-aa50-694c5097d55b> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "This carpet was initially on long term loan to the South Kensington Museum (today called the V&A), from 1883, and it would be over twenty years before the museum officially acquired it, in 1910. At that time, the owner was the art-collector George Salting, who loaned so much (particularly Chinese porcelain) to the museum, that a dedicated gallery was organised just to display his collection. After Salting’s death in 1909, the collection was bequeathed to the V&A on a permanent basis. The carpet was discussed as “the Salting Carpet” in later scholarship (see below), but it was not the only carpet in the Salting Bequest."@en .

